<h2 id="use-cases">Best Use Cases</h2>
<h3>Aidoc Excels At:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Emergency Department Triage:</strong> Real-time detection and prioritization of critical findings in trauma and acute care</li>
<li><strong>Stroke Management:</strong> Rapid LVO detection enabling faster thrombectomy and better neurological outcomes</li>
<li><strong>Trauma Centers:</strong> Simultaneous screening for multiple traumatic injuries requiring immediate intervention</li>
<li><strong>After-Hours Radiology:</strong> AI backup during nights and weekends when radiologist coverage is limited</li>
<li><strong>Pulmonary Embolism Triage:</strong> Immediate PE detection for timely anticoagulation therapy</li>
<li><strong>Neurosurgical Emergencies:</strong> ICH and cervical spine fracture detection for rapid surgical consultation</li>
<li><strong>Quality Assurance:</strong> Safety net to prevent missed critical findings due to oversight or fatigue</li>
<li><strong>Multidisciplinary Care Coordination:</strong> Real-time alerts enable immediate specialist consultation</li>
</ul>
<h3>May Not Be Ideal For:</h3>
<ul><li>Outpatient imaging centers without emergency cases</li><li>Small hospitals with very low trauma volumes</li><li>Facilities focused only on elective imaging</li><li>Organizations unable to support enterprise implementation</li></ul>
<h2 id="comparison">How It Compares</h2>
<h3>Aidoc vs Zebra Medical Vision</h3>
<p>Aidoc specializes in real-time triage of acute, life-threatening conditions with immediate mobile alerts, while Zebra Medical Vision offers broader multi-condition screening for incidental findings. Aidoc excels in emergency department and trauma settings with faster notification systems optimized for critical care. Zebra provides wider disease coverage across chronic and acute conditions. Aidoc's strength is speed and specialty-specific workflows for time-critical cases, while Zebra's advantage is comprehensive screening breadth. Many hospitals deploy both - Aidoc for emergency triage and Zebra for comprehensive population health screening.</p>
<h3>Aidoc vs Viz.ai</h3>
<p>Both excel at stroke detection and time-critical triage, but Aidoc offers broader specialty coverage with 11+ algorithms across neurology, pulmonary, trauma, and more. Viz.ai specializes in stroke care coordination with particularly strong workflow integration for thrombectomy programs. Aidoc provides more comprehensive emergency department support across multiple pathologies, while Viz.ai focuses on optimizing stroke and PE care pathways end-to-end. For stroke-specific programs, Viz.ai may have an edge; for comprehensive ED AI support, Aidoc offers more versatility. Both are excellent platforms with different optimization focuses.</p>
<h2 id="verdict">Final Verdict</h2>
<div class="verdict-box">
<h3>Our Recommendation</h3>
<p>Aidoc is the premier AI triage platform for emergency departments, trauma centers, and acute care hospitals where rapid detection and treatment of critical conditions saves lives. The platform's 24/7 always-on monitoring and instant mobile alerts have demonstrably reduced time-to-treatment for stroke, pulmonary embolism, and traumatic injuries across hundreds of hospitals. With 11+ FDA-cleared algorithms covering major emergency specialties, Aidoc provides comprehensive AI support for high-acuity settings. The real-time notification system ensures critical findings immediately reach the right clinicians, regardless of time or location. For Level 1 trauma centers and comprehensive stroke centers, Aidoc's impact on patient outcomes justifies the significant enterprise investment. The platform's specialty-specific worklists and workflow optimization tools measurably improve radiologist efficiency during high-volume periods. Implementation requires substantial IT resources and organizational commitment, but hospitals with appropriate infrastructure report excellent ROI through improved quality metrics, reduced door-to-treatment times, and enhanced patient safety. The always-on nature means every scan receives AI analysis without requiring radiologist activation, providing a crucial safety net during busy shifts and after-hours coverage. While enterprise pricing limits accessibility to smaller facilities, major health systems and academic medical centers will find Aidoc an essential component of modern emergency care. If your organization handles significant acute care volumes and prioritizes rapid critical finding detection, Aidoc represents a best-in-class solution with proven clinical benefits. Request a demonstration to see the platform's specialty-specific capabilities and discuss customized deployment for your institution's needs.</p>
</div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">What conditions does Aidoc detect?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">Aidoc's FDA-cleared algorithms detect time-critical conditions across 11+ specialties including intracranial hemorrhage, large vessel occlusion (stroke), pulmonary embolism, cervical spine fractures, rib fractures, incidental pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, and other acute pathologies. The platform focuses specifically on life-threatening or time-sensitive findings that require immediate clinical intervention. Additional algorithms are continuously being developed and validated for new conditions and specialties.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">How fast are Aidoc's alerts?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">Aidoc provides real-time analysis, typically delivering alerts within minutes of scan completion. The platform processes studies as they're acquired, analyzing images during reconstruction. When critical findings are detected, push notifications are sent immediately to configured smartphones and devices. Studies show Aidoc reduces time from scan completion to radiologist notification by 60-80% compared to standard workflows, enabling faster treatment initiation for time-sensitive emergencies like stroke and trauma.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Is Aidoc FDA approved?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">Yes, Aidoc has received FDA 510(k) clearance for multiple algorithms including intracranial hemorrhage detection, cervical spine fracture detection, pulmonary embolism detection, and other indications. Each algorithm undergoes rigorous clinical validation and regulatory review before clearance. Aidoc also has CE marking for use in Europe. The platform meets regulatory requirements as a medical device for clinical diagnostic support and is deployed in compliance with HIPAA and other healthcare data protection regulations.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Does Aidoc replace radiologists?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">No, Aidoc is designed to assist radiologists, not replace them. The platform serves as a triage and prioritization tool that flags urgent cases requiring immediate attention. Radiologists remain responsible for final diagnosis, clinical reporting, and patient care decisions. Aidoc's value is in ensuring critical findings don't get delayed in busy workflows and providing a safety net against oversight. Studies show AI-assisted radiology combining Aidoc's triage with radiologist expertise delivers optimal patient outcomes compared to either alone.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">What infrastructure is needed for Aidoc?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">Aidoc requires integration with your hospital's PACS system to receive imaging studies and deliver results. The platform can be deployed cloud-based or on-premise depending on your security and performance requirements. Cloud deployment needs reliable internet for study upload and result delivery. You'll also need smartphones or mobile devices for clinicians to receive push notifications. Aidoc's implementation team provides technical support for PACS integration with major vendors including GE, Philips, Siemens, and others. Implementation typically takes several weeks to months depending on complexity.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">How much does Aidoc cost?<span>+</span></div><div class="faq-answer">Aidoc uses custom enterprise pricing based on hospital size, scan volumes, selected algorithms, and deployment configuration. Pricing models typically include annual licensing fees with options for per-study charges or unlimited usage. Costs generally range from hundreds of thousands to over a million dollars annually for large health systems. The investment includes software licensing, PACS integration, mobile app access, ongoing technical support, and algorithm updates. Many hospitals report strong ROI through improved patient outcomes, reduced liability from missed findings, and enhanced operational efficiency. Contact Aidoc directly for pricing tailored to your organization's specific requirements.</div></div>
</article>
